I"<p>Digital badges support educational goals when the institution connects an assessed outcome to explicit criteria, evidence and an issued achievement—not when it simply awards an attractive image.</p>

<h2 id="choose-the-achievement-before-the-badge">Choose the achievement before the badge</h2>

<p>Start with a learning outcome that can be observed. Specify the task, assessment method, minimum standard and approving team. Attendance and demonstrated competence are different achievements; the badge description should say which one was recognized.</p>

<p><img class="img-fluid r-16" src="/img/blog/digital-badges-impact-on-learning-goals.png" alt="Impact of digital badges on learning goals" style="display: block; margin: 0 auto;" /></p>

<h2 id="use-an-outcomeevidencerecognition-checklist">Use an outcome–evidence–recognition checklist</h2>

<p>For each badge, record: the outcome in the programme’s language; the assessment artifact or protected evidence reference; the assessor’s decision; the issuer; the award date; and any applicable expiry or status rules. For assessed capabilities, use <a href="/skills-taxonomy-mapping">institution-reviewed skills mapping</a> rather than treating a badge label as proof of every related skill.</p>

<p><img class="img-fluid r-16" src="/img/blog/badge-credibility-pyramid.png" alt="Credibility of badges" style="display: block; margin: 0 auto;" /></p>

<h2 id="recognise-a-research-methods-milestone">Recognise a research-methods milestone</h2>

<p>A department could recognize a learner’s completed research proposal after rubric-based assessment. The credential could identify the achievement and criteria while keeping confidential feedback in an authorized system. This example is a programme design pattern, not a claim about a named customer or a ready-made integration.</p>

<p><img class="img-fluid r-16" src="/img/blog/embracing-digital-badges.png" alt="Embracing of digital badges through steps" style="display: block; margin: 0 auto;" /></p>

<h2 id="use-a-standard-where-it-fits-the-programme">Use a standard where it fits the programme</h2>

<p>An <a href="/blog/why-institutions-should-embrace-open-badges-3-0-standards">Open Badges 3.0 achievement credential</a> can describe issuer, achievement and evidence. Confirm the supported proof mechanism and receiving-system compatibility. A digital badge image alone does not establish conformance or interoperability.</p>

<h2 id="keep-the-learners-record-coherent">Keep the learner’s record coherent</h2>

<p>Related achievements can contribute to a <a href="/comprehensive-learner-record">Comprehensive Learner Record with issuer provenance</a>. Decide who may see evidence, how corrections are handled and whether the achievement remains meaningful after a programme changes.</p>

<h2 id="evaluate-educational-usefulness-not-just-issuance-volume">Evaluate educational usefulness, not just issuance volume</h2>

<p>Review assessment completion, learner understanding and the burden on academic staff. Sharing and page views measure engagement, not skill attainment or employment. The <a href="/platform-overview">institutional credential lifecycle</a> helps teams agree these controls before rollout. See <a href="/case-studies.html">anonymous implementation evidence</a> for documented examples without assuming the same results.</p>

<h2 id="plan-your-next-step">Plan your next step</h2>

<p>Discuss the actual award, evidence and implementation requirements with your institution. <a href="https://info.certifyme.online/request-demo">Request an institutional demo</a>.</p>

